# The `logweir-ui` image — Task 39. THE PAGE, AS AN IMAGE INSTEAD OF A ConfigMap.
#
# IT IS NOT A THIRD LOGWEIR BINARY AND IT COMPILES NOTHING. `Dockerfile` builds
# the runner (two binaries, the extracted OSO engine, an amd64-only ELF);
# `Dockerfile.weirkeeper` builds the controller (one binary, `ca-certificates`
# and nothing else). This file builds neither: it is a `COPY` of twenty-two
# static files over a kubectl the Kubernetes project publishes, and its whole
# build is two layers and a second or two. There is no builder stage, no
# `rustup`, no `apt`, no `$BUILDPLATFORM` argument and no cross-compile
# refusal, because there is nothing here to compile — which is also why this
# image, unlike the controller's, can be built for either architecture on
# either kind of runner.
#
# BUILD IT WITH THE RECIPE, NOT BY HAND:
#
#     just image-ui                            # this host's architecture, loaded
#     LOGWEIR_IMAGE_PLATFORM=linux/amd64 just image-ui
#
# and then assert it:
#
#     bash scripts/check-image-ui.sh logweir-ui:check; echo "rc=$?"
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE PAGE MOVED OUT OF THE CHART
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Until Task 39 `charts/logweir` carried its own byte-identical copy of `ui/`
# (fourteen files, ~86 KB of JavaScript, HTML and CSS), rendered it into a
# ConfigMap through `.Files.Glob "ui/**"` and mounted that into the proxy pod.
# Three costs, all of them paid on every install:
#
#   * a second copy of the page in the repository, kept honest only by a `cmp`
#     loop in `scripts/check-chart.sh` — a copy a contributor could edit on one
#     side and a gate that could only ever say "they differ", never "these are
#     the bytes the browser gets";
#   * the fourteen files serialised into one API object on every `helm
#     template`, `helm install` and `helm upgrade`, inside etcd, inside every
#     rendered file checked into `charts/logweir/rendered/`;
#   * a ConfigMap is a mutable object in the cluster, so "the page the browser
#     loads" was whatever anyone with `patch configmaps` had last written.
#
# An image is the opposite of all three: one copy in the tree, resolved by
# digest, immutable once pushed. And the assertion gets STRONGER rather than
# weaker — `scripts/check-image-ui.sh` computes the sha256 of every file the
# image will serve and of every file in `ui/`, and compares them. That is a
# statement about the bytes an operator's browser receives; the chart's old
# `cmp` arm was a statement about two directories in this repository.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS *NOT* HERE, AND EACH ABSENCE IS A DECISION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ARGUMENTS STAY IN THE CHART. `kubectl proxy`'s flags — and in particular
# `--accept-paths='^/(ui/|apis/logweir\.dev/v1alpha1/)'` — are a SECURITY
# PROPERTY: that regex is the authorisation boundary between a page that may
# read the six kinds and a page that may read Secrets. Three tests in
# `crates/logweir/tests/chart_lint.rs` assert it against the rendered
# Deployment. Baking those flags into an `ENTRYPOINT`/`CMD` here would move the
# boundary into a layer those tests cannot see and an operator cannot read from
# `helm template`. So the ENTRYPOINT stays the base image's `kubectl` and the
# chart keeps every argument.
#
# NO `ui/README.md` AND NO `ui/tests/`. The globs below take `*.html`, `*.js`
# and `*.css` and the `pages/` directory; the README is documentation and
# `ui/tests/` carries a THROWAWAY KEYPAIR and fixtures naming a developer's
# compose stack (Global Constraint 28: no key material in the page or in
# anything that serves it). `scripts/check-image-ui.sh` asserts `/ui` holds the
# twenty-two files and NOTHING else, so an image that gained either is a red.
#
# NO SHELL, NO PACKAGE MANAGER, NO `apt` LAYER. The base carries `/bin/kubectl`
# and a distroless root filesystem — measured 2026-09-14: `docker run
# --entrypoint /bin/sh` on it fails with `stat /bin/sh: no such file or
# directory`. That is why `scripts/check-image-ui.sh` reads every file with
# `docker create` + `docker cp` onto the host (the `scripts/extract-engine.sh`
# idiom) instead of `test -s` inside the image, the way its two siblings do.
#
# NO `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`, and that is the same argument
# `Dockerfile.weirkeeper` makes about the MIT notice. That file is the
# inventory of the RUST dependency graph, generated from `Cargo.lock`; this
# image links no Rust binary and redistributes no crate, so shipping it would
# claim a redistribution that does not happen. `check-image-ui.sh` asserts it
# is ABSENT. What this image DOES redistribute is kubectl, and the file it owes
# for that is the inventory below.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BASE, PINNED BY DIGEST — GLOBAL CONSTRAINT 7
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `registry.k8s.io/kubectl` at the digest `charts/logweir/values.yaml` pinned
# for `ui.image` until this task, resolved from `registry.k8s.io/kubectl:v1.34.1`
# on 2026-09-12 (`charts/logweir/README.md` records the command and the date).
# It is a MANIFEST LIST — linux/amd64, linux/arm64, linux/ppc64le, linux/s390x,
# measured 2026-09-14 — so the same digest builds on either runner
# `.github/workflows/release.yml` uses, and each build resolves to that
# architecture's own variant.
#
# A DIGEST-PINNED `FROM` CANNOT SILENTLY RESOLVE TO OTHER BYTES, which is worth
# stating because it decides what `check-image-ui.sh`'s base check has to do:
# the only drift possible is between this line, the LABEL below and the
# checked-in inventory, and that is exactly the three-way agreement that script
# asserts. It does not re-download the base to compare layers, and it says so.
FROM registry.k8s.io/kubectl@sha256:59bafa07ff3a6d4b417e7633ddb9d79a9606ca98bf64bac080b3e65748669250

# THE BASE, DECLARED IN THE IMAGE'S OWN METADATA. `org.opencontainers.image.base.name`
# is the OCI annotation for exactly this, and it is what lets an operator who
# has the image but not this file answer "what is under my UI?" with
# `docker image inspect`. `scripts/check-image-ui.sh` check 4 reads it back and
# holds it to the `FROM` above.
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.base.name="registry.k8s.io/kubectl@sha256:59bafa07ff3a6d4b417e7633ddb9d79a9606ca98bf64bac080b3e65748669250"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="logweir-ui"
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.description="The Logweir page, served by kubectl proxy --www=/ui. Twenty static files and nothing else."
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.licenses="Apache-2.0"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWENTY-TWO FILES, AND NOTHING ELSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO INSTRUCTIONS, NOT ONE `COPY ui /ui`. A whole-directory copy would take
# `ui/README.md` and `ui/tests/` with it — the second of which carries a
# throwaway keypair — and would keep taking whatever lands under `ui/` next.
# These globs take the twelve top-level files (`*.html`, `*.js`, `*.css`) and
# the nine under `pages/`, which is `scripts/check-ui-offline.sh`'s scope and
# `shipped_ui_files()`'s in `crates/logweir/tests/chart_lint.rs`.
#
# Docker's `*` does not cross a `/`, so `ui/*.js` is the eleven top-level scripts
# and never `ui/tests/*.js`.
COPY ui/*.html ui/*.js ui/*.css /ui/
COPY ui/pages /ui/pages

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE LICENCES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOGWEIR'S OWN, for the page: the twenty-two files above are Logweir's code under
# Apache-2.0, and Apache-2.0 requires the licence and the NOTICE to travel with
# the redistribution (Global Constraint 15, spec §16 clause 5). The same path
# both other images use, so one `docker run --entrypoint cat` idiom finds them
# in any of the three.
COPY LICENSE NOTICE /usr/share/licenses/logweir/

# AND KUBECTL'S, because this image REDISTRIBUTES kubectl — the whole base
# layer, binary included — the same way the runner image redistributes
# `kafka-backup` and ships the MIT notice for it in
# `/usr/share/licenses/kafka-backup/`.
#
# TWO FILES. `INVENTORY` names the base by digest, names its licence and says
# where its text is; `LICENSE` is that text. kubectl is Apache-2.0, the same
# licence Logweir's own code carries, so the text is one file in this tree
# (`LICENSE` — the unmodified Apache-2.0 with the appendix's placeholders
# intact) copied to both places rather than two identical files checked in
# twice. The copyright holders differ and the INVENTORY is where that is
# written down; the licence TEXT is what Apache-2.0 §4(a) requires a copy of.
COPY third_party/kubectl-image.inventory /usr/share/licenses/kubectl/INVENTORY
COPY LICENSE /usr/share/licenses/kubectl/LICENSE

# THE SAME NON-ROOT UID AS BOTH OTHER IMAGES AND AS the chart's own PodSpec
# (`runAsUser: 65532`, `runAsNonRoot: true`). The base declares `USER 0`; the
# chart's `securityContext` overrides it, but an image whose default is root is
# an image that runs as root wherever the PodSpec is forgotten. The files above
# are copied mode 0644 owned by root and are world-readable, which is what a
# static file server needs and all it needs.
USER 65532:65532

# THE ENTRYPOINT THE BASE ALREADY SETS, RESTATED SO THIS FILE SAYS WHAT THE
# IMAGE RUNS. No `CMD`: every argument is the chart's (see the header), and a
# default argument vector here would be a second place the proxy's path filter
# could come from.
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/kubectl"]
